<section class="container section"><div class="eyebrow">YOUR WORKSPACE / THE GROWTH KIT</div><div class="section-heading"><div><h1 class="page-title">A good idea.<br><em>A better experiment.</em></h1><p class="lede">Your kit is unlocked. Pick one change and make it measurable.</p></div><a class="button outline" href="{{ route('onboarding') }}">← My checklist</a></div>
<div class="kit-grid"><article class="panel"><span class="pill">01 / COPY TEMPLATE</span><h2>Make your promise clear.</h2><p>Fill in this structure before rewriting your product page:</p><blockquote>For [specific customer] who wants [outcome], [product] helps you [benefit] without [friction].</blockquote><p><strong>Example:</strong> For small-space gardeners who want fresh herbs, our windowsill kit helps you grow your first harvest without a backyard.</p><p>Place one honest proof point below it. Use a product detail, a real review, or a clear return policy.</p></article><article class="panel"><span class="pill">02 / OFFER TEMPLATE</span><h2>Make the next step easy.</h2><blockquote>Get [specific result] with [what’s included]. Start with [small next action].</blockquote><ul class="feature-list"><li>Name the full price before checkout.</li><li>Explain delivery and returns clearly.</li><li>Use a CTA that describes the next step.</li><li>Keep one primary action per section.</li></ul></article></div>
<div class="panel experiment-panel"><span class="pill">03 / YOUR 7-DAY EXPERIMENT</span><h2>Change one thing. Learn something useful.</h2><div class="steps-grid"><article><span>DAY 1–2</span><h3>Observe & choose</h3><p>Record visitors, signups, and purchases. Pick one friction point and write a hypothesis: “If we clarify delivery, more visitors will start checkout.”</p></article><article><span>DAY 3–6</span><h3>Run the change</h3><p>Change only that element. Keep the offer and traffic sources consistent. Record each day’s counts without declaring a winner early.</p></article><article><span>DAY 7</span><h3>Review & repeat</h3><p>Calculate signups ÷ visitors and paid ÷ visitors. Compare the same periods. Small samples are directional; collect more data before making a decision.</p></article></div></div></section>
